'''Horaci Eduard Molí, S.A.''' is a Balboan {{w|integrated textile mill}} headquartered in [[Sant Cristòfor]] with production facilities in [[Sant Bart]]. The company operates a vertically integrated textile manufacturing operation, processing synthetic fibers into woven and knitted fabrics, technical textiles, and finished garments. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker HEM and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

The company sources {{w|polyester}}, {{w|nylon}}, and {{w|acrylic fiber}} from domestic suppliers, including [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] and [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]], and processes them into finished textile products for both domestic and export markets. Its production facilities in Sant Bart cover the full manufacturing chain from yarn spinning through weaving, knitting, dyeing, finishing, and garment assembly.

== History ==

Horaci Eduard Molí was founded in 1972 by Horaci Vilanova i Mas and Eduard Cabot i Riera, two textile industry veterans who had previously managed weaving operations for a canal corridor garment manufacturer. The two founders identified an opportunity to build a domestic integrated textile mill that could process locally produced synthetic fibers into finished fabrics, reducing Balboa's dependence on imported textiles.

The company established its first production facility in [[Sant Bart]] in 1973, taking advantage of the town's rail connections to both the [[Badia Curta]] chemical cluster — where fiber producers such as [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]] and [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] would later source or produce their feedstocks — and the canal corridor export terminals at [[Sant Cristòfor]]. The initial facility housed spinning, weaving, and dyeing operations.

During the 1980s, the company expanded its Sant Bart complex, adding knitting lines and a garment assembly division. The expansion allowed Horaci Eduard Molí to offer a full vertical chain from fiber to finished product, positioning the company as a supplier to both domestic apparel retailers and export buyers.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1990. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] acquired a 5 per cent stake in 2005 as part of its broader investment in the domestic manufacturing value chain.

In the 2010s, Horaci Eduard Molí invested in {{w|technical textile}}s, developing fabrics for industrial applications including filtration, automotive interiors, and protective clothing. The technical textiles division now accounts for approximately 15 per cent of revenue.

== Operations ==

The company's headquarters in [[Sant Cristòfor]] houses administration, sales, and design functions. All production is concentrated at the Sant Bart complex, which comprises:

* Yarn spinning lines processing synthetic fiber from [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] and [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]]
* Weaving machines producing woven fabrics
* Knitting machines producing knitted fabrics
* Dyeing and finishing facilities
* Garment assembly workshops
* Technical textiles division

Approximately 60 per cent of output is exported, primarily to [[Tapuya]], [[Potocsí]], [[Balisca]], and [[BTPMEU]] member states. The remainder supplies domestic apparel manufacturers and retailers.

== Supply chain ==

Horaci Eduard Molí occupies the downstream end of the Badia Curta synthetic fiber value chain:

* [[Companyia Química Occidental]] and other chemical producers supply {{w|purified terephthalic acid|PTA}}, {{w|ethylene glycol|MEG}}, {{w|caprolactam}}, and {{w|acrylonitrile}} to fiber producers
* [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]] produces polyester chips and filaments from these feedstocks
* [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] produces polyester, nylon, and acrylic fiber
* Horaci Eduard Molí processes these fibers into woven fabrics, knitted fabrics, technical textiles, and finished garments

